    <item>
      <title>OAuth flow</title>
      <link>https://docs.uitsmijter.io/oauth/flow/</link>
      <pubDate>Mon, 01 Jan 0001 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate>
      <guid>https://docs.uitsmijter.io/oauth/flow/</guid>
      <description>&lt;div class=&#34;gdoc-page__anchorwrap&#34;&gt;&#xA;    &lt;h1 id=&#34;oauth-flow&#34;&gt;&#xA;        OAuth flow&#xA;        &lt;a data-clipboard-text=&#34;https://docs.uitsmijter.io/oauth/flow/#oauth-flow&#34; class=&#34;gdoc-page__anchor clip flex align-center&#34; title=&#34;Anchor to: OAuth flow&#34; aria-label=&#34;Anchor to: OAuth flow&#34; href=&#34;#oauth-flow&#34;&gt;&#xA;            &lt;svg class=&#34;gdoc-icon gdoc_link&#34;&gt;&lt;use xlink:href=&#34;#gdoc_link&#34;&gt;&lt;/use&gt;&lt;/svg&gt;&#xA;        &lt;/a&gt;&#xA;    &lt;/h1&gt;&#xA;&lt;/div&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;OAuth 2.0 is an open standard for authorization that enables third-party applications to obtain limited access to a&#xA;user&amp;rsquo;s resources without requiring the user to provide their login credentials.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;There are several flows that can be used with OAuth 2.0, depending on the type of application and the&#xA;resources it needs to access. Here is a brief overview of the most common flows that ist supported by Uitsmijter:&lt;/p&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Grant Types</title>
      <link>https://docs.uitsmijter.io/oauth/granttypes/</link>
      <pubDate>Mon, 01 Jan 0001 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate>
      <guid>https://docs.uitsmijter.io/oauth/granttypes/</guid>
      <description>&lt;div class=&#34;gdoc-page__anchorwrap&#34;&gt;&#xA;    &lt;h1 id=&#34;grant-types&#34;&gt;&#xA;        Grant Types&#xA;        &lt;a data-clipboard-text=&#34;https://docs.uitsmijter.io/oauth/granttypes/#grant-types&#34; class=&#34;gdoc-page__anchor clip flex align-center&#34; title=&#34;Anchor to: Grant Types&#34; aria-label=&#34;Anchor to: Grant Types&#34; href=&#34;#grant-types&#34;&gt;&#xA;            &lt;svg class=&#34;gdoc-icon gdoc_link&#34;&gt;&lt;use xlink:href=&#34;#gdoc_link&#34;&gt;&lt;/use&gt;&lt;/svg&gt;&#xA;        &lt;/a&gt;&#xA;    &lt;/h1&gt;&#xA;&lt;/div&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;In OAuth 2.0, the term “grant type” refers to the way an application gets an access token.&#xA;Uitsmijter supports several grant types.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;div class=&#34;gdoc-page__anchorwrap&#34;&gt;&#xA;    &lt;h2 id=&#34;configuration&#34;&gt;&#xA;        Configuration&#xA;        &lt;a data-clipboard-text=&#34;https://docs.uitsmijter.io/oauth/granttypes/#configuration&#34; class=&#34;gdoc-page__anchor clip flex align-center&#34; title=&#34;Anchor to: Configuration&#34; aria-label=&#34;Anchor to: Configuration&#34; href=&#34;#configuration&#34;&gt;&#xA;            &lt;svg class=&#34;gdoc-icon gdoc_link&#34;&gt;&lt;use xlink:href=&#34;#gdoc_link&#34;&gt;&lt;/use&gt;&lt;/svg&gt;&#xA;        &lt;/a&gt;&#xA;    &lt;/h2&gt;&#xA;&lt;/div&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Grant types can be set at &lt;code&gt;Client&lt;/code&gt; level.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;div class=&#34;highlight&#34;&gt;&lt;pre tabindex=&#34;0&#34; class=&#34;chroma&#34;&gt;&lt;code class=&#34;language-yaml&#34; data-lang=&#34;yaml&#34;&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;line&#34;&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;cl&#34;&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;w&#34;&gt;  &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;nt&#34;&gt;grant_types&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;p&#34;&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;w&#34;&gt;&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;line&#34;&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;cl&#34;&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;w&#34;&gt;    &lt;/span&gt;- &lt;span class=&#34;l&#34;&gt;authorization_code&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;w&#34;&gt;&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;line&#34;&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;cl&#34;&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;w&#34;&gt;    &lt;/span&gt;- &lt;span class=&#34;l&#34;&gt;refresh_token&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;w&#34;&gt;&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;line&#34;&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;cl&#34;&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;w&#34;&gt;    &lt;/span&gt;- &lt;span class=&#34;l&#34;&gt;password&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;w&#34;&gt;&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;line&#34;&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;cl&#34;&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;w&#34;&gt;    &lt;/span&gt;- &lt;span class=&#34;l&#34;&gt;device_code&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;w&#34;&gt;&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;p&gt;If none of any grant type is specified, that &lt;code&gt;authorization_code&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;refresh_token&lt;/code&gt; are enabled by default.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Available Endpoints</title>
      <link>https://docs.uitsmijter.io/oauth/endpoints/</link>
      <pubDate>Mon, 01 Jan 0001 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate>
      <guid>https://docs.uitsmijter.io/oauth/endpoints/</guid>
      <description>&lt;div class=&#34;gdoc-page__anchorwrap&#34;&gt;&#xA;    &lt;h1 id=&#34;available-endpoints&#34;&gt;&#xA;        Available Endpoints&#xA;        &lt;a data-clipboard-text=&#34;https://docs.uitsmijter.io/oauth/endpoints/#available-endpoints&#34; class=&#34;gdoc-page__anchor clip flex align-center&#34; title=&#34;Anchor to: Available Endpoints&#34; aria-label=&#34;Anchor to: Available Endpoints&#34; href=&#34;#available-endpoints&#34;&gt;&#xA;            &lt;svg class=&#34;gdoc-icon gdoc_link&#34;&gt;&lt;use xlink:href=&#34;#gdoc_link&#34;&gt;&lt;/use&gt;&lt;/svg&gt;&#xA;        &lt;/a&gt;&#xA;    &lt;/h1&gt;&#xA;&lt;/div&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;An endpoint is a specific location capable of accepting incoming requests and is usually a specific URL&#xA;(Uniform Resource Locator) that is provided by an API (Application Programming Interface). An API is a set of&#xA;programming instructions and standards for accessing a web-based software application or web tool. APIs allow different&#xA;software systems to communicate with each other, and enable functionality such as requesting data from a server, or&#xA;sending data to a server for storage.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>JWT Decoding</title>
      <link>https://docs.uitsmijter.io/oauth/jwt_decoding/</link>
      <pubDate>Mon, 01 Jan 0001 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate>
      <guid>https://docs.uitsmijter.io/oauth/jwt_decoding/</guid>
      <description>&lt;div class=&#34;gdoc-page__anchorwrap&#34;&gt;&#xA;    &lt;h1 id=&#34;jwt-decoding&#34;&gt;&#xA;        JWT Decoding&#xA;        &lt;a data-clipboard-text=&#34;https://docs.uitsmijter.io/oauth/jwt_decoding/#jwt-decoding&#34; class=&#34;gdoc-page__anchor clip flex align-center&#34; title=&#34;Anchor to: JWT Decoding&#34; aria-label=&#34;Anchor to: JWT Decoding&#34; href=&#34;#jwt-decoding&#34;&gt;&#xA;            &lt;svg class=&#34;gdoc-icon gdoc_link&#34;&gt;&lt;use xlink:href=&#34;#gdoc_link&#34;&gt;&lt;/use&gt;&lt;/svg&gt;&#xA;        &lt;/a&gt;&#xA;    &lt;/h1&gt;&#xA;&lt;/div&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;OAuth does not have a specific standard &amp;ldquo;profile endpoint&amp;rdquo; for decoding the content of a JSON Web Token (JWT). OAuth is&#xA;a standard for authorization, which means that it is primarily concerned with granting and revoking access to resources,&#xA;rather than with providing information about the user who is accessing the resources.&#xA;But certain libraries (like &lt;code&gt;oidc-client-ts&lt;/code&gt;) expecting it for decoding a profile content, so Uitsmijter supports&#xA;a decoding endpoint for valid and non expired tokens at &lt;code&gt;GET /token/info&lt;/code&gt;. See more details at&#xA;the &lt;a&#xA;  class=&#34;gdoc-markdown__link&#34;&#xA;  href=&#34;https://docs.uitsmijter.io/oauth/endpoints&#34;&#xA;&gt;endpoint documentation&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>PKCE</title>
      <link>https://docs.uitsmijter.io/oauth/pkce/</link>
      <pubDate>Mon, 01 Jan 0001 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate>
      <guid>https://docs.uitsmijter.io/oauth/pkce/</guid>
      <description>&lt;div class=&#34;gdoc-page__anchorwrap&#34;&gt;&#xA;    &lt;h1 id=&#34;authorization-code-flow-with-proof-key-for-code-exchange&#34;&gt;&#xA;        Authorization Code Flow with Proof Key for Code Exchange&#xA;        &lt;a data-clipboard-text=&#34;https://docs.uitsmijter.io/oauth/pkce/#authorization-code-flow-with-proof-key-for-code-exchange&#34; class=&#34;gdoc-page__anchor clip flex align-center&#34; title=&#34;Anchor to: Authorization Code Flow with Proof Key for Code Exchange&#34; aria-label=&#34;Anchor to: Authorization Code Flow with Proof Key for Code Exchange&#34; href=&#34;#authorization-code-flow-with-proof-key-for-code-exchange&#34;&gt;&#xA;            &lt;svg class=&#34;gdoc-icon gdoc_link&#34;&gt;&lt;use xlink:href=&#34;#gdoc_link&#34;&gt;&lt;/use&gt;&lt;/svg&gt;&#xA;        &lt;/a&gt;&#xA;    &lt;/h1&gt;&#xA;&lt;/div&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;The Authorization Code Flow with Proof Key for Code Exchange (PKCE) is an OAuth 2.0 flow that is used to secure the&#xA;authorization process in native applications and single-page applications (SPAs). It is an extension to the standard&#xA;authorization code flow, and it adds a step that helps to prevent authorization code interception attacks.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>OAuth Scopes and Scope Filtering</title>
      <link>https://docs.uitsmijter.io/oauth/scopes/</link>
      <pubDate>Mon, 01 Jan 0001 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate>
      <guid>https://docs.uitsmijter.io/oauth/scopes/</guid>
      <description>&lt;div class=&#34;gdoc-page__anchorwrap&#34;&gt;&#xA;    &lt;h1 id=&#34;oauth-scopes-and-scope-filtering&#34;&gt;&#xA;        OAuth Scopes and Scope Filtering&#xA;        &lt;a data-clipboard-text=&#34;https://docs.uitsmijter.io/oauth/scopes/#oauth-scopes-and-scope-filtering&#34; class=&#34;gdoc-page__anchor clip flex align-center&#34; title=&#34;Anchor to: OAuth Scopes and Scope Filtering&#34; aria-label=&#34;Anchor to: OAuth Scopes and Scope Filtering&#34; href=&#34;#oauth-scopes-and-scope-filtering&#34;&gt;&#xA;            &lt;svg class=&#34;gdoc-icon gdoc_link&#34;&gt;&lt;use xlink:href=&#34;#gdoc_link&#34;&gt;&lt;/use&gt;&lt;/svg&gt;&#xA;        &lt;/a&gt;&#xA;    &lt;/h1&gt;&#xA;&lt;/div&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;OAuth 2.0 scopes are a mechanism to limit an application&amp;rsquo;s access to a user&amp;rsquo;s resources. Scopes define what permissions an application has and what data it can access on behalf of the user.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Uitsmijter implements a &lt;strong&gt;two-tier scope filtering system&lt;/strong&gt; that provides fine-grained control over which scopes are granted to users:&lt;/p&gt;</description>
    </item>
